Dave

Here is a snippet:

ld_mmap: Could not malloc 206160. bytes, for
libD/OSF_4_0_ALPHA/libAgUiP.a(AgUiSnHfPwrI.o),  didnt attempt to mmap
ld_mmap: Could not malloc 206160. bytes, for
libD/OSF_4_0_ALPHA/libAgUiP.a(AgUiSnHfPwrI.o),  didnt attempt to mmap
file                                 file_offset / flen -- pg bndry file
offst --> last file offst mmapd
mmap usage info not available; max of 160. slots
Fatal error in: /usr/lib/cmplrs/cc/ld IOT/Abort trap
...failed Link binDS/OSF_4_0_ALPHA/PlFireSatVO ...



In the past, these types of messages indicated one (or more) of several
possible situations:

insufficient swap/virtual memory
process limit set too low for text
process limit set too low for data



There is another set of messages that also appear at times:

swap space below 10 percent free
Unable to obtain requested swap space
Unable to obtain requested swap space
cc: Fatal: Virtual memory limit or system swap space exhausted.
Try reducing program size or system load, or increase swap space
(see swapon(8)), adjust system config file entries maxdsiz and maxvas.


Perhaps what is needed is to re-build the kernel to set user
process limits higher for "maxdsiz" and "maxvas", but before
doing so, it would be nice to know what the "maxvas" parameter
is.   The "maxdsiz" is the process limit for data size, which
we have already adjusted at boot time with the use of "limit"
in one of the rc scripts.


